{"kind":"request","text":"claude: a design note on running more than one workroom in one repository. No code, and please push back on the framing if it is wrong.\n\nHugh wants the chess implementation to have its own workroom inside the chess repository, so the repo both holds the game and records the work that built it. Multiple independent genesis roots in one repository was deliberately scoped out earlier; the chess lane is where it has become load-bearing.\n\nWhat I found reading the code, offered as a starting point and not as a conclusion, because you should verify it rather than inherit it. The storage layout already namespaces everything by genesis: kernel.go 292 refs/seq/\u003cgenesis\u003e, checkpoint.go 474 refs/gitseq/checkpoints/\u003cgenesis\u003e, resident.go 86 refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e. Two workrooms would not collide in git. The constraint looks like one line of bookkeeping: app.go 382-384 derives a single metaDir per repository holding one config.json with one Genesis, and Init refuses with \"workroom already initialized\" when it exists. AttachConfig at app.go 500-539 already takes and validates a genesis argument, so the concept of naming which workroom you mean partly exists.\n\nThe half I am less sure of, and the half that matters more. rests_on cannot cross logs -- provenance resolution refuses with \"causal reference does not resolve in this log\" -- and there is no global URL form; the genesis prefix distinguishes a root without fetching it. Artifacts look like an escape hatch and I do not think they are: kinds.go 174 requires only that path and commit be present, both plain strings, and I could not find anything verifying the commit exists in the repository. If that is right, a workroom can record an artifact naming a commit no reader there can check, which is the failure this project exists to prevent, and it should be said out loud in the note whether or not it is fixed. Check it; I would rather be corrected than have that sentence propagate.\n\nWhy you, and the honest caveat. You wrote the notes-and-decisions note, took six review rounds on it, and the corrections that came out of those rounds are the reason it is worth reading. This is the same kind of piece. But you are carrying several lanes today and this is not urgent -- the chess workroom is empty and nothing is blocked on it this week. Decline or defer it the way ox-builder deferred the UI addresses request and I will treat that as the right call, not a gap.\n\nTwo things adjacent to this that I am not asking you to solve. Reviewer independence in a second workroom -- two agents or reviews come back to this roster. And 820f993d, the external-repository landing contract, whose premise assumes chess lands from this workroom; if multi-genesis is the answer, that request needs re-deriving rather than answering.","body":{"conditions":"A design note under notes/ settles, at source rather than from documentation: what changes to make more than one workroom co-exist in one repository, and what the boundary between two logs in one repository is and is not. It states the current single-workroom constraint precisely, naming the file and the refusal. It says how a command selects which workroom it acts in, and what happens when the selection is absent or ambiguous -- refusing is an acceptable answer if argued. It covers the resident, which binds one genesis today, and says whether one resident serves several workrooms or each gets its own. It states plainly what does NOT become possible: whether rests_on can cross logs, and if not, what an author should write instead when a decision in one log depends on one in another, including what staleness coverage is lost by that substitution. It examines whether an artifact naming a commit absent from the repository is admitted today, and says whether that should change. It names the affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md and whether their contracts change. Nothing here is built: the note is the deliverable, it makes no code change, and it publishes an artifact at its own path resting on the behaviour it describes. Review requested from an actor other than planner, as its own durable request.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}